  "basis": "Named PMTiles Spean Thmor bridge POI17592756541329, about280m northeast of the older catalog coordinate, agrees with the21July2025 photo location. No exact-QID outline or dimensional survey is present. Heading follows the nearby older east-west road corridor and the inferred photo-facing south side; approximate rather than independently surveyed. The8-bay,16.8m visible-remnant interpretation and broken outer extent are provisional reconstruction controls; photographs establish stepped corbels, broken slabs and two root systems, not a measured complete bay schedule.",
  "limits": [
    "Only the photographed visible remnant segment is interpreted: six passages with partial upper corbels/slabs and two broken eastern channels/pier stubs. The full surviving ruin footprint, total bay count and hidden masonry remain unverified. This source draft is not a completed unique-candidate model.",
    "Two tree/root silhouettes are photo-led approximations, not botanical surveys. Portable GLBs use small opaque modeled leaf sprigs rather than alpha-card crowns; this avoids per-model atlases and unsupported portable shader bindings. Shared native scatter cards remain the standard for ordinary generated forest.",
    "Stone blocks, irregular course heights, explicitly drawn creeping root paths and tree proportions are authored deterministically. The current model remains only a visible-remnant draft. No downloaded mesh or copied photo pixels. Native ground and both modern crossings must remain unchanged."
  ],
  "rootPathBasis": "Explicit photo-led branching/creeping root paths replace the uniform radial armature. Paths crawl over the retained paving, overlap and split at block edges, and descend with flattened, curved buttresses. The wider ordinary Hello Angkor image IMG_20200622_124315-2 shows that the collapsed remnant extends farther than the current source draft; this remains an explicit full-scope blocker. Ken Marshall south-side photo4070 (2011, camera13.446008/103.879483) and TravelSafeBC2019 front photograph show a low-forked, knotted pale tree at the front edge, with a taller dark tree rooted on retained paving. These replace the earlier two uniform tall poles."
